# Egress-exception template. Copy this file to .powbox-egress (gitignored) in the
# powbox checkout to let every agent container this checkout launches reach a
# few destinations the firewall otherwise blocks: private (10/8, 172.16/12,
# 192.168/16), link-local (169.254/16, fe80::/10), unique-local (fc00::/7) and
# CGNAT (100.64/10, the range Tailscale assigns). Everything else is already
# allowed. With no .powbox-egress, or one holding only blank and # lines, the
# default policy applies unchanged.
#
# One rule per line, like a hosts file:
#
#   <proto> <destination> <port>
#
#   - proto: tcp or udp.
#   - destination: an IPv4 or IPv6 address (one host) or a CIDR. Host bits past
#     the prefix must be zero, and a /0 is refused.
#   - port: one port number, or `any`. Write one line per port.
#
# A # starts a comment that runs to the end of the line. A malformed line stops
# the launch with a message naming the line. A destination the default already
# allows, or one that reopens at least half of a blocked range, is accepted
# with a warning.
#
# Changes apply to new containers: a stopped container whose rules differ is
# recreated on the next launch, and a running one must be stopped first. Inside
# a container, $POWBOX_EGRESS_ALLOW lists the active exceptions. The rules need
# a base image built with egress-exception support; an older one ignores them
# and keeps the destinations blocked.
#
# Examples (uncomment and adapt):
#
# A forward proxy on one tailnet host:
# tcp 100.101.102.103 3128
#
# The same host over the tailnet's IPv6 address:
# tcp fd7a:115c:a1e0::1234 3128
#
# A development database on the LAN:
# tcp 192.168.1.20 5432
#
# DNS on a LAN resolver, both transports:
# udp 192.168.1.1 53
# tcp 192.168.1.1 53
